- 'pending invoices' / 'invoices pending approval' / 'invoices awaiting approval' / 'unapproved invoices' -> target_entity 'Invoice' with a 'pending' filter whose value is true. This is ApprovalStatus='Required' — the default reading of "pending" in AP. The answer must name the reading and mention the alternatives (pending control check, pending payment) so the user can correct in one turn.

- RELATIVE PENDING: 'invoices pending for a long time' / 'long-pending invoices' / 'invoices stuck in approval' / 'invoices pending too long' / 'invoices pending for a while' — where the prompt names pending approval AND an open-ended age but gives NO explicit duration — -> target_entity 'Invoice' with a 'pending' filter (true) AND an 'older_than_n_months' filter whose value is {"months": 3}. Three months is the default age window; the answer must disclose it and offer to change it. Do NOT set unanswerable=true for a vague age: the user is asking about long-pending invoices, and refusing converts English into a SQL-shaped demand. Do NOT route to 'created_in_last_n_months' — that is the recent window, the opposite direction.

- PENDING WITH EXPLICIT AGE: 'invoices pending for over N months' / 'invoices pending for more than N days' / 'invoices pending for N weeks' / 'invoices pending for N years' — where the prompt carries an explicit N — -> target_entity 'Invoice' with a 'pending' filter (true) AND an 'older_than_n_months' filter whose value is {"months": N} (convert days and weeks to whole months by rounding up: 30 days -> 1, 90 days -> 3, 180 days -> 6; convert years to months: 1 year -> 12, 2 years -> 24). Routes to the invoice_date_before tool arg; the planner computes the ISO cutoff from N. Do NOT compute the date yourself.

- 'invoices pending in the last N months' / 'invoices pending in the last N days' / 'recently pending invoices' -> target_entity 'Invoice' with a 'pending' filter (true) AND a 'created_in_last_n_months' filter whose value is {"months": N}. Routes to the invoice_date_after tool arg. The distinction from RELATIVE PENDING matters: 'for a long time' means an OLDER window; 'in the last N months' means the RECENT window.
